Indexing into a slice returned from a method.
==============================================

Pinned 2026-05-05. `h.args()[1]` builds a TIndex(TCall("Holder_args"),
1, string). The TIndex codegen for SliceType pushed the index, then
called `genExpr(array)` which left a 24-byte slice ret slot on the
stack between the pushed index and the new r7. Two bugs ganged up:

1. `pshd r1` for the index didn't update `stackOffset`, so the inner
   `Holder_args` TCall's `retSlotOffset` was off by 8 — it wrote
   `slice.ptr` at offset 8 instead of offset 0 of its ret slot, and
   the ldd/std for the backref bled past the slot's end into the
   pushed index's memory.
2. After `genExpr(array)` returned (with r1 pointing into the slice
   ret slot, r7 24 bytes lower), `popd r2` read from r7 — i.e., from
   inside the ret slot — instead of from where the index was pushed.
   The "index" became `slice.ptr`, the bounds check failed against a
   pretend "len = some huge stack address", and either the next
   instruction faulted or the wrong element was returned.

Test_args_zero accidentally passed before fix #2 because reading
slice.ptr at offset 0 of a *malformed* descriptor still gave the
heap pointer for `pos`, and `slice.ptr + 0*16` was the correct first
element address by luck.

Fix in `SyslTriscCodegen.case TIndex`: track stackOffset around
pshd/popd and reclaim any extra space genExpr left between the index
slot and r7 before popd. Same shape as the regArg "extra reclaim" in
TCall — the slice ret slot's data lives below r7 after reclaim, but
nothing writes there before the next ldd reads ptr/len out of it.

Applies to all four TIndex variants: StringType, SliceType, RefType,
default (ArrayType / scalar). Runtime regression below; the codegen
shape is covered in `SyslTriscMethodOnTempTests`.

    module sysl.tests.positional_min

    import std.debug.{assert}

    struct Holder
        items: []string
        count: int

    Holder.args() -> []string = self.items

    new_holder() -> Holder
        var pos = (new [4]string)[:0]
        pos = append(pos, "foo")
        pos = append(pos, "bar")
        Holder(pos, 2)

    #test
    test_args_zero() -> unit
        val h = new_holder()
        assert(h.args()[0] == "foo", "args[0]=foo")

    #test
    test_args_one() -> unit
        val h = new_holder()
        assert(h.args()[1] == "bar", "args[1]=bar")

    #test
    test_args_both() -> unit
        val h = new_holder()
        assert(h.args()[0] == "foo", "args[0]=foo")
        assert(h.args()[1] == "bar", "args[1]=bar")
